In the accelerator pedal release phase, the ANOVA shows a statistically significant main effect of AR warnings on the distance between the driver and the pedestrian crossing (D 1 ) (F = 3.832, p = 0.024, partial eta squared = 0.239, observed power = 0.897). Specifically, both AR systems were very effective in informing the driver, well in advance, of the potential danger of crossing a pedestrian; this prompted the driver to release the accelerator pedal approximately 10 m earlier than in the baseline condition. Although not significant, the TTC 1 and TTZ 1 were quite high in both AR configurations, confirming the effectiveness of the warnings. As expected, no significant effect of the AR warnings was recorded for S 1 .

In the pressure on the brake pedal phase, the ANOVA shows the main effect of the AR warnings on all variables. It was observed that drivers started braking well in advance, with lower speed and higher TTC 2 and TTZ 2 , which translates into safer behaviour and manoeuvres and demonstrates the effectiveness of AR warnings with respect to pedestrian crossing.

Finally, in the deceleration phase , the statistical analysis shows the significance of AR warnings over the distance D 3 between the driver and the pedestrian crossing, where the maximum deceleration was recorded (F = 5.238, p = 0.007, partial eta squared = 0.349, observed power = 1.000). In addition, it was observed that, in the AR configurations, drivers adopted lower average and maximum decelerations, resulting in conservative deceleration manoeuvres.
5.3. Hidden pedestrian
5.3.1. Accident

The results demonstrated a significant improvement in road safety with the application of AR warning systems. The percentage of accidents that occurred under the baseline condition was 31 %; this percentage can be considered relatively high, demonstrating the importance of simulated crossings with a hidden pedestrian under very critical conditions. In both AR configurations, this percentage was considerably reduced; in the visual warning AR configuration, it was only 2%; in the audio-visual warning configuration, the percentage was zero. The same promising results are confirmed by the percentage of drivers who did not yield to the pedestrian: in the baseline condition it was low but still 5%; the resulting percentage was zero in both AR configurations. Therefore, based on these overall results, it has been shown that AR systems are very effective in identifying an unseen pedestrian, thus avoiding a possible collision.
